<Doc
  title="Workspace: files, search, source control, terminal and logs"
  description="Browse and edit files, search, work with Git and pull requests, or open a shell on a connected project from the private dashboard and the Chrome side panel."
  canonicalPath="/docs/workspace/"
>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Workspace</strong> in the dashboard. Choose a project, then a workspace, from the
    dropdowns at the top of that page. Source Control and the terminal are available only to the
    signed-in owner of that project, while the machine that holds the selected workspace is
    connected. Project pages still offer <strong>Open workspace</strong>, which lands on the same
    page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selector">The selector</h2>

  <p>
    A workspace is a branch with a working copy of its own, in one{" "}
    <a href="/docs/projects/">location</a> of the project. The selector names each one by its branch
    and the location that holds it, because the same branch can have a working copy in two
    locations and the slug alone does not say which is which.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first entry is the <strong>project root</strong> of the default location, marked{" "}
    <code>default branch</code>: the working copy a call lands in when it names no workspace. It
    shows the branch that copy is really on, which is not necessarily <code>main</code>. Every
    other location that holds a copy has a root too, listed as <code>root</code> with the name of
    the location, and it opens like any other entry.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every other entry is a workspace: a Git worktree on one of your machines, or an instance on
    Exeora Cloud. An instance that is still being set up is listed with its state and cannot be
    chosen until it is ready. One that is asleep is woken when you select it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="views">The views</h2>

  <p>
    Workspace has six views. On a wide screen they are the icons down the left edge, with the
    view's list beside them and what you pick from the list open as tabs on the right: a single
    click opens a preview tab that the next click replaces, a double click keeps it. On a phone,
    and in the Chrome side panel, the same views are the bar along the bottom, the list fills the
    screen, and what you pick opens over it with Back. Explorer is <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>,
    Search <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>, Source Control <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each view needs a CLI that can serve it. Source Control as it always was works with any CLI;
    the Explorer, Search, the commit history, stashes and the assistant's context need one that
    announces <code>workspace-v2</code> and <code>source-control-v2</code>. A view whose machine runs
    an older CLI says so and names the machine, while the others keep working. Instances on Exeora
    Cloud are always current.
  </p>

  <h2 id="explorer">Explorer</h2>

  <p>
    The files of the working copy, a folder at a time: only the root and the folders you open are
    listed, so a repository with a large dependency tree costs nothing until that folder is opened.
    Files matched by <code>.gitignore</code> are hidden unless you show them; <code>.git</code> is
    never listed and never touched. Files and folders carry the icons of VS Code's Material Icon
    Theme, by name and extension, here and in Search and Source Control. What git says about a
    file is drawn beside it, and on the folders above it, in the same letters Source Control uses.
  </p>

  <p>
    A file opens in an editor with syntax colouring. Save with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>
    or the button: the editor sends the version it read along with the new text, and a file that
    changed on the machine meanwhile is not overwritten; the banner offers to reload it or to
    overwrite anyway. Markdown files open as a page with a way back to the source, images open as
    pictures, and a file over a megabyte is shown but not edited. A file with changes offers its
    diff from the editor's toolbar.
  </p>

  <p>
    A right click, or a long press on a touch screen, gives every row its actions: new file or
    folder, rename, move, duplicate, copy its path, delete. On a wide screen a row can also be
    dragged into a folder. Renaming or moving a tracked file uses <code>git mv</code>, so the
    rename shows as staged. Deleting is permanent on that machine: there is no trash to recover
    from, which the confirmation says.
  </p>

  <h2 id="search">Search</h2>

  <p>
    Search runs on the machine that holds the workspace, across every file git would track, as you
    type. It can match case, whole words, or a regular expression, and be narrowed to files to
    include or exclude by glob. Hits are grouped by file, or under their folders, and a hit opens
    the file on its line. A search that stops at the limit says so.
  </p>

  <p>
    Replace applies to one hit, one file or every hit at once, keeping the case of an all-caps or
    capitalised match when asked. Each file is replaced only if it is still the version that was
    searched; one that changed meanwhile is skipped and named. A file with unsaved edits open in
    the editor is never replaced under it. Replace all is withheld while the results are cut short,
    since it would miss what is not listed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source-control">Source Control</h2>

  <p>
    The dashboard reads Git's porcelain status from the selected workspace and presents it as a git
    client: the current branch in the toolbar, a commit composer, what is staged and what changed,
    as a list or grouped by folder. <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the message commits, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>
    starts a body. The one button under the message names what the situation calls for: commit
    what is staged, publish a branch that has no upstream, sync when the branch is both ahead and
    behind, pull, push, stage what changed. Everything else git can do here waits under the
    button's chevron: commit and push, commit and sync, amend the last commit, stage or unstage
    everything, discard every change, fetch, pull, push, sync, publish, stash and stash pop. An
    amend of a commit that is already on the upstream, and any discard, ask first.
  </p>

  <p>
    A row's menu stages, unstages, discards, deletes an untracked file or copies its path; a
    folder's does the same for everything under it. The whole working tree, or everything staged,
    opens as one diff. On a wide screen a diff can be shown side by side. <strong>Auto refresh</strong>
    reads the status and the open diff again every three seconds while Source Control is on
    screen, and pauses while the browser tab or side panel is hidden. Turn it off to stop polling,
    or use <strong>Refresh</strong> for a one-off update.
  </p>

  <p>
    Commits shows the history behind HEAD with its graph, a page at a time. A commit opens with its
    message and the files it changed with what each gained and lost; a file opens its diff for that
    commit, and the whole commit opens as one patch. Stashes are listed with a way to apply or drop
    each.
  </p>

  <p>
    The branch control lists the current branch, other local branches, and remotes you can check
    out. Choosing a branch that is already checked out in another connected workspace switches the
    view to that workspace rather than running <code>git switch</code> in place. Type a name there
    to create a branch from HEAD, or delete a merged local branch.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same control creates a workspace. Type a branch and choose <strong>Create workspace</strong>:
    the dialog asks where, and offers every location of the project, starting on the one you are
    looking at. It says what the choice will do before you confirm it. On a machine that already
    holds a copy it adds a worktree. On a machine that holds none it clones the repository there
    first, which can take a few minutes. On Exeora Cloud it starts an instance, which counts
    against the plan's limit. A machine that is offline cannot take a workspace until{" "}
    <code>exeora connect</code> is running on it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Exeora invokes the installed <code>git</code> executable directly, without passing these actions
    through a shell. Fetch, pull and push use the credentials git has on that machine, and
    interactive credential prompts are disabled so an unattended operation fails instead of
    hanging. For a project <a href="/docs/github/">connected to GitHub</a>, git on an instance, and
    on a machine whose own credentials could not read the repository, asks Exeora for a
    short-lived token instead. Sync is a pull followed by a push; a pull that needs a choice
    between merging and rebasing fails with git's own words, and is made from the terminal.
  </p>

  <p>
    For projects registered from a repository subdirectory, status, diffs, staging and destructive
    actions stay inside that subdirectory. A commit is refused while the Git index contains staged
    files outside the selected project root.
  </p>

  <p>
    Force push, hard reset, remote branch deletion, forced local branch deletion, and exposed rebase
    controls are intentionally absent. Pull uses that repository's own Git configuration.
  </p>

  <p>
    With an assistant <a href="/docs/ai-assist/">linked in Settings</a>, a button beside the
    message writes it from what is staged, and <strong>Ship changes</strong> under the chevron goes
    from a working tree to a pull request in one go.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pull-request">Pull Request</h2>

  <p>
    The pull request of the branch on screen, read with the GitHub account
    <a href="/docs/github/">connected in Settings</a>; the details are on
    <a href="/docs/pull-request/">their own page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Web terminal</h2>

  <p>
    Opening a terminal requires an explicit confirmation. It starts the machine's default shell in a
    real pseudo-terminal at the selected workspace's path on the machine that holds it. Open
    terminals belong to the
    dashboard as a whole: the list stays in the chrome when you leave Workspace, switch project, or
    reload the tab. Closing the browser detaches the dashboard; the local process keeps running
    until you close that terminal, the CLI disconnects, the machine is revoked, or the session
    reaches its idle or maximum lifetime. Reopening the dashboard reconnects to the same shell.
  </p>

  <p>
    A terminal connection uses a one-time ticket that expires after 30 seconds and is bound to this
    site's origin and selected workspace. One web terminal may be open per workspace, so the project
    root and multiple connected workspaces can each have one. Idle sessions end after 30 minutes and
    every session ends after eight hours.
  </p>

  <p>
    The terminal sits in the Workspace frame like every other view: beside the column of views on a
    wide screen, above the bar of views on a phone and in the side panel, so any other view is one
    click away while the shell keeps running.
  </p>

  <h2 id="logs">Logs</h2>

  <p>
    Logs shows the calls agents make on the selected root or workspace as they run, the way
    <code>exeora connect</code> prints them on the machine: an arrow when a call starts, with the
    tool, what it is about and the client that made it, then a tick or a cross when it ends, with
    how long it took and the error code of a call that failed. What a call is about is the command
    it runs, the pattern it searches for, the path it reads or writes, or the arguments of a
    proxied MCP tool. Several calls can be running at once; the header counts them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing in Logs is kept. The lines go to the tabs watching at that moment and are gone when the
    tab closes: a tab opened later sees the calls still running, not the ones that already ended.
    Switching to another view and back keeps the lines that arrived meanwhile, and switching the
    root or workspace starts over. Watching never wakes an Exeora Cloud instance; the calls do.
    Like a terminal, the connection uses a one-time ticket bound to this site's origin and to the
    selected root or workspace, and it reconnects on its own when the network drops.
  </p>

  <h2>What is recorded</h2>

  <p>
    Source Control mutations, every change the Explorer or a replace makes, generations by an
    assistant, pull request actions and terminal session openings appear in the audit stream, by
    name only. Exeora does <strong>not</strong> record command text, keystrokes, terminal output,
    file contents, diffs, search queries or commit messages. What Logs shows of a call's
    arguments is shown to you while you watch and written down nowhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Project policy</h2>

  <p>
    Every view of Workspace is an owner-operated feature. None of them travels through an MCP
    client, so none is subject to the project's MCP command policy: a read-only project still lets
    its owner edit a file from the Explorer. Opening
    the terminal grants the same authority as opening a local shell as the user running the CLI;
    review the confirmation accordingly.
  </p>
</Doc>
